From: Alie Jie Li <alisonjieli@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 09:16:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Restore the second round of the starter catalogue review

The second round of independent review judged the 74 starter items that
main's review record left without a verdict and approved 71 of them. It was
recorded on 21 September 2026 in the worktree of release/catalogue-round-two,
staged there and never committed, because the recording agent stopped at a
usage limit. Main carried 43 approved items; it now carries 114.

What was taken, and from where: the rows of the 74 round two items in the
staged reviews.json of that worktree (HEAD ade90f0), byte for byte for each
decision, reason, outcome, rule, approval reference and the digest and size
of the bytes the reviewers read. The staged diff is identical to the
September 22 archive patch apart from abbreviated index hashes. Patches of the
staged record and of the version three draft in restore-d18-catalogue-pay are
saved in /home/username/.le-safety/catalogue-round-two-20260923/ with their
SHA256SUMS. The round's decisions also equal the three reviewers' own outputs
in the workflow journal (wf_2c5a17d0-bee): 74 verdicts from each, no
mismatch; each rejection reason is the reviewer's text behind the reviewer
label. Nothing else of that worktree is taken: its manifest generator,
container check and host release implemented the withholding rule that
main's carry tool replaced.

Review record version three. Version two named one revision of reviewed bytes
for the whole record, so it could not hold a second round read at another
revision. Version three names each round with the date, the revision and
folder that hold the bytes its reviewers read, the anchor revision those
bytes name and the items it judged: round one at 81f341d (anchored at
381efec, 49 items) and round two at ade90f0 (anchored at 0cf19eb, 74 items).
Every judged row belongs to exactly one round. The carry tool reads each
round's bytes at that round's revision, and the manifest generator refuses a
carried approval whose reviewed revision is not its own round's anchor. Both
tools refuse versions one and two. The tool changes are the reviewed design
of the draft in restore-d18-catalogue-pay, ported onto main, with a round
number that must be an integer and identities that must be names.

The approvals, checked against the bytes: all three named reviewers judged
each of the 74 items; 71 carry three approvals and no objection, and 3 carry
one written objection from reviewer three (two near duplicates of approved
items, and respond_to_a_leaked_credential, whose first step withdraws a live
credential with no declared authority); each recorded digest is the sha256
of the body at ade90f0, which is byte for byte the body in the main checkout
at f89b720 that the reviewers state they read; every item declares MIT, and
the reviewer records say none of them produced an item under review (the
items were written earlier by the pay/catalogue-expand agent). All 71
approvals carry from the 0cf19eb bytes to today's bodies at 565e133 under
carry_catalogue_approvals.py, none refused, and the 43 of round one are
unchanged. The host release is rebuilt: 114 items.

The known defect of the draft: its REVIEW.md bullet named revision ade90f0,
which the rule source_references_are_pinned refuses because the sheet may
name only anchor revisions. With that bullet, test_the_committed_catalogue_
passes_every_rule failed on that rule; the bullet now points at the revision
reviews.json names for round two and the check passes. A new known-wrong case
("the review sheet names the revision a round of review read") reads the
record's round revisions and plants one in the sheet, so the rule's mutant
control covers this exact defect.

The homepage library count and the README count move from 43 to 114; the
homepage count is held to the release manifest by
test_the_library_count_is_this_release_manifest_count.

